          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l04",
          "reminder": "Use a sign chart and retain the endpoints allowed by the original inequality."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The expression factors as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its zeros are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and 2. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the three factors are negative, giving a negative product. The signs then alternate at these three distinct roots, so the product signs are −,+,−,+ on the four successive intervals.</p><p>It is positive precisely on <strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, option E.</strong> The roots themselves give zero and are excluded by the strict inequality.</p>",
